WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:03.460
Film Courage: How many feature scripts have
you written?

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:06.970
Jeremy Foley, Filmmaker/Actor/Screenwriter:
Oh gosh, let me think… feature scripts we

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:14.860
have written…one…two ( I have to count)…probably
seven or eight feature scripts and then probably

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:25.620
oh two or three television or teleplay scripts
you know shorter like pilots or you know our

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:35.590
podcast script or so so probably about a dozen
scripts in total and then within those dozen

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:42.610
completed scripts you know certainly we have
a you know a giant board with all of our ideas

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:48.820
and then we have a lot of treatments for things
that we've written and we've written a lot

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:55.350
of you know we've written a lot of pitch decks
for a lot of our projects and we've written

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:01.950
a lot of series bibles or series pitches for
a lot of our television projects so yeah a

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:07.940
lot of a lot of writing a lot of writing but
yeah we think I would say about a dozen probably

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.960
feature screenplays and when you first started
writing these features who would you send

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:21.390
them to for reading you know when we first
started writing features I think there was

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:27.270
probably probably about there was there was
two major things we would do I guess I would

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:35.210
say or I think would recommend for people
I mean certainly like your friends if if you

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:41.820
have friends in the industry fantastic that's
great send them to those friends and even

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.460
if you don't have friends in the industry
still anybody who's willing to read it I would

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:51.571
say send it to them because it can it can
take people a while to read a screenplay and

00:01:51.571 --> 00:01:57.950
get back to you but also I think what was
really helpful for us in the early days is

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:02.300
you can find there's there's many of them
online the you know there are screenwriting

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:09.759
services or screenwriting consultancy agencies
and screenwriting contests things like that

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:14.790
but I think what was really helpful for us
and we first started we would do a lot of

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:22.890
those because I think what's helpful is the
turnaround time as I said like you can of

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.420
course and and I recommend getting the scripts
to your friends and family and people like

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.380
that or people in the industry if you know
it but of course it can really take people

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:39.650
a while to read a screenplay think about it
get back to you you know we're all busy everybody's

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:44.970
you know doing something or doing their job
or they've got their own scripts so I think

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:51.420
a big benefit in the early days for us of
sending it to those screenwriting like coverage

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:58.420
places or consulting agencies was just the
turnaround time you were able to get it in

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:04.620
you know sometimes it's it's quick sometimes
it's you know 72 hours or a week or two weeks

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:10.709
and I think especially when you're at least
for us when you're just starting out having

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:21.480
that instant feedback or or quick turnaround
feedback was really helpful because we could

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:26.830
keep up the momentum of our script you know
what I mean like when you're writing it like

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:34.300
being able to get notes quickly would help
us just get back into a draft and really either

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.390
keep on shaping it or keep adjusting things
that maybe didn't work so well or and maybe

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:44.280
if we did hear that they were working well
you know we would we would leave them and

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:50.960
I think another benefit to sending it to those
agencies you know those screenwriting competitions

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.890
or consulting consulting agencies was you
know you're just doing it blind like if you

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:02.440
send it to your family or your friends they
know you maybe perhaps they even know the

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:07.900
story of the screenplay and when you send
it to those people it's just you know it's

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:18.419
anonymous so it's like I think they can give
you sometimes you know brutal feedback or

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:23.509
honest feedback about what's really working
or what's not working and they're not necessarily

00:04:23.509 --> 00:04:30.450
as concerned about you know your feelings
or or how you know the story or you know they

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.659
don't necessarily know that it was maybe something
that happened to you or something from your

00:04:34.659 --> 00:04:40.150
past or whatever it is they really just focus
on that script so I would suggest that and

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:46.999
I think that's a really helpful thing to do
and I know that helped us a lot in the beginning

00:04:46.999 --> 00:04:55.610
do you think Hollywood's closed off I don't
think Hollywood's closed off I think I think

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:02.080
you know again because I've been doing this
for so long I think Hollywood and I've been

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:09.319
in the business 25 years 25 or 20 plus years
you know I think it's more open now than probably

00:05:09.319 --> 00:05:17.830
I've ever seen it before because of social
media I think you know I know social media

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:24.159
is like a double-edged sword like you know
you have outrage over game of thrones episodes

00:05:24.159 --> 00:05:31.279
but you can also like really I've seen a lot
of people like really legitimately advance

00:05:31.279 --> 00:05:37.759
their careers as actors or screenwriters or
directors whatever it is because they're they're

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:43.610
just releasing stuff online they're able to
find an audience and then use that to kind

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:51.149
of you know catapult themselves into something
else or to find a representation as a as a

00:05:51.149 --> 00:05:59.729
writer or as an actor director and I think
another thing actually is kind of cool about

00:05:59.729 --> 00:06:05.399
you know Hollywood becoming more open and
with social media too you know you have things

00:06:05.399 --> 00:06:10.849
like crowdfunding that never existed before
and I've you know we've crowdfunded ourselves

00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:16.539
and it's just been like an amazing resource
and we were able to do so much more for our

00:06:16.539 --> 00:06:23.499
films because we did have we were able to
generate those funds so no I don't think Hollywood

00:06:23.499 --> 00:06:29.889
is closed off I think that this is probably
the most open I've ever seen it are you okay

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:36.490
if the two of you never get a feature film
made will you be okay with shorts and little

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:46.629
you know web series and things like that no
no to answer your question I we are really

00:06:46.629 --> 00:06:52.589
always striving for that feature film that
is really important to us you know important

00:06:52.589 --> 00:06:59.629
to me and important to Sara my partner you
know we we love film and we love television

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:04.529
and we've made a lot of really wonderful short
films films that we're really proud of and

00:07:04.529 --> 00:07:10.389
we're gonna continue making them we're gonna
continue writing scripts and making webisodes

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:17.479
and podcasts but there's one thing that we
really haven't fully tackled yet and I don't

00:07:17.479 --> 00:07:24.289
think we can really cross that off cross our
list you know our bucket list so to speak

00:07:24.289 --> 00:07:30.419
our filmmaking bucket list until we hit the
future and so that's something we're still

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:35.029
pushing towards and I think you know we've
gotten close many many times and obviously

00:07:35.029 --> 00:07:40.789
the the pandemic that we've all just went
through and that we're starting to come out

00:07:40.789 --> 00:07:47.939
of now is sort of through everybody for a
for a loop I know a lot of filmmaker friends

00:07:47.939 --> 00:07:53.360
and actor friends and I know a lot of people's
projects were sort of put on hold some people

00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:00.580
were able to continue working but but I think
yeah I think that made a lot of people lose

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:07.369
momentum so I think now that everything's
back I think we're ready to to you know throw

00:08:07.369 --> 00:08:13.689
our hat in the ring again and go after the
feature next and are you too cautious in in

00:08:13.689 --> 00:08:17.259
terms of you want to do it right like you
know I see people do amazing crowdfunding

00:08:17.259 --> 00:08:21.489
campaigns but then it turns out oh we didn't
raise enough we've got to go back and I know

00:08:21.489 --> 00:08:27.659
that's difficult yeah to and it happens it
it happens with everything in life it's this

00:08:27.659 --> 00:08:33.010
amount the time I couldn't I couldn't even
tell you how many stories I've seen about

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:40.440
people making features that have taken them
years literally years to make one feature

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:47.060
where they you know shoot something and then
a year later come back and shoot something

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:50.589
else and these aren't documentary features
where you can you know these are narrative

00:08:50.589 --> 00:08:56.550
feature films where you're using the same
actors for you know sometimes years at a time

00:08:56.550 --> 00:09:03.300
I've heard so many stories like that and and
so yeah we are a little bit cautious and the

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:09.970
films that Sara and I enjoy making and the
things we like to do are kind of high concept

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:14.890
a little bit complex or a little bit complicated
there's a lot of usually there's a lot of

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:21.839
moving parts in our short films and in in
our webisodes and in things like that so we

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:29.279
are cautious I don't think we're ready we're
I don't think we're going to jump into a feature

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:37.470
until we kind of feel like we have you know
there's you're never going to feel completely

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:44.440
comfortable you're always no matter what you
do I think making films you're always there

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:50.560
there's always a an unknown you know always
always and there's always going to be a million

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:58.870
problems every day but yeah we are we are
being a little bit cautious about exactly

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:04.930
how we're going to jump into a future but
I i'm hoping that you know from the experiences

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:12.190
we've had on all of our shorts and just with
all of the festivals we've been to you know

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:22.509
I think we can kind of see a little more of
of where it could go wrong and so we're it's

00:10:22.509 --> 00:10:29.529
it's really going to be about trying to kind
of course correct ahead of time so it doesn't

00:10:29.529 --> 00:10:35.120
go wrong while we're in the moment do you
have a date a target date like

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:45.190
2025 the feature will be finished you know
we I we don't have a target date of when we're

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:52.000
trying to make a feature and I feel like at
least for me personally I've kind of tried

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:57.100
to get over that idea that I need to have
it done at a certain time or I need to be

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:05.010
a certain age or I need to you know x, y,
and z needs to fall into place before I can

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:16.910
go out and do it i'm trying not to let myself
get distracted by that but I do want to focus

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:23.990
first on making it excellent and also making
it realistic something that we can accomplish

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:28.870
and accomplish well you know set out to do
and really execute it at a very high high

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:38.420
level but you know for whatever budget we're
able to to to get or to finance it for I guess

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:44.990
if that makes sense like I want to make sure
like we're we're being realistic and that

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:51.959
we're just gonna we're really going to deliver
that so that's more important to to me and

00:11:51.959 --> 00:11:54.832
to us than to put a ticking clock on it.

00:11:54.832 --> 00:11:55.832
Question For The Viewers:

00:11:55.832 --> 00:11:55.839
How many feature films have you written?

